@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product;design] sound-no-ringing-controls
No control plays a ringing or pitched glass tone. A tap, a select and a toggle are a dry
click and a short knock, gone in a few hundredths of a second; ringing tones are only for
outcomes such as success ("This sound, I hate it ... never use that sound for anything",
rule 125; J3, J16).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:architecture;product] sound-cue-per-role
Each kind of control has one cue and one vibration, given by the kit (`cueClickable`,
`cueCombinedClickable`); a screen MUST NOT pick its own. Every fold toggles the same way
and every hold is the app's one hold, never the system's buzz (rule 120; J20).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product] sound-every-cue-is-felt
Every control that plays a cue also vibrates with the phone's own short effects: a tick
for a quiet button or a choice, a click for a main button or a switch, two for a success,
a heavy one for a failure. A swipe ticks when letting go would act and again if taken
back; a page that changes under the finger ticks (rule 109; H18).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product] sound-follows-the-phone
Sounds play at the phone's touch-sound volume, stay silent in silent and vibrate modes and
take no audio focus; vibration follows the phone's touch feedback setting and the app's
own Vibration switch (rule 109).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product;security] feedback-follows-current-account-preferences
The process-wide sound and haptics services MUST read the current account settings
for each cue, rather than retain the preference owner selected during Application
startup. Switching back to ExistingServer MUST restore that flow's own feedback
choices. Hardware initialization MAY remain one-shot. Source: Tavi's 25 Oct 2026
